+# Rainbow mobile deep-linking on "Stake" — what's happening and how to fix it
+
+## TL;DR
+
+The transaction request never depended on the deep link. WalletConnect ships it
+over an encrypted relay socket; the deep link only **foregrounds the wallet** so
+the user sees the prompt. Before the patch, that foregrounding was lost because
+it fired after an `await` (outside the tap's user-activation window). The patch
+"fixed" the foregrounding by opening Rainbow *before the request was published*,
+so the wallet came forward with nothing queued. The fix is to open the wallet
+**after** the request is on the relay, target the **wallet's own redirect scheme
+from the WC session metadata** (not a hardcoded `rainbow://`, not `connector.id`),
+and **skip the deep link entirely** for injected / in-app-browser / desktop cases.
+
+---
+
+## (1) Why nothing auto-opened before the patch
+
+WalletConnect v2 is a **relay protocol**, not a deep-link protocol. When you call
+`writeContractAsync`, wagmi's WalletConnect connector does this:
+
+1. Encodes the call into an `eth_sendTransaction` JSON-RPC request.
+2. Encrypts it against the paired session's symmetric key.
+3. **Publishes it to the WC relay** (a WebSocket relay server). Rainbow, holding
+   the other half of the session, receives it over its own relay subscription and
+   surfaces the signing prompt.
+
+That path is exactly why "if they manually switch to Rainbow, the request is
+waiting." The request **arrived fine**. Nothing about signing needs a deep link.
+
+The deep link does one job: **bring the wallet app to the foreground** so the user
+doesn't have to alt-tab. RainbowKit normally does this by reading the wallet's
+`redirect` metadata (captured during the pairing handshake) and navigating to it
+right after dispatching the request.
+
+So why did it silently do nothing on mobile? Because of the **user-activation
+requirement**. iOS Safari (and mobile browsers generally) only allow a page to
+launch another app (`window.location = scheme:` / `window.open`) when the
+navigation happens **synchronously inside a user-gesture handler** — within a few
+hundred ms of the tap, before control returns to the event loop.
+
+`writeContractAsync` is `async`. Encoding + encrypting + publishing to the relay
+all happen across `await` points. By the time the connector is ready to fire the
+redirect, the gesture's activation window has expired, so the OS **silently
+blocks the app switch**. The page "just sits there." The request, meanwhile, went
+out over the relay independently and is waiting in Rainbow.
+
+**Key mental model:** request delivery (relay) and wallet foregrounding (deep
+link) are two independent channels. Only the second one was failing.
+
+---
+
+## (2) Why the patch made it worse
+
+```js
+window.location.href = "rainbow://";   // line 1
+await writeContractAsync(...);          // line 2
+```
+
+This inverts the ordering. It foregrounds Rainbow **before the request exists**:
+
+- `rainbow://` (bare scheme, no WC payload) just opens Rainbow to its home
+  screen. There is no pending request yet — `writeContractAsync` hasn't run.
+- Worse, navigating to `rainbow://` **backgrounds the browser tab**. On iOS a
+  backgrounded page is frozen: timers throttled, JS suspended, the WebSocket may
+  be torn down. So `writeContractAsync` often never finishes publishing while the
+  page is in the background. The request is never put on the relay at all.
+
+Net effect: Rainbow opens instantly (good UX illusion), but there is genuinely
+nothing to sign, and the transaction never lands onchain. The patch traded a
+"delivered but not foregrounded" bug for a "foregrounded but never delivered" bug.
+
+---
+
+## (3) The correct pattern
+
+### Ordering
+
+Dispatch the write **first**, let the request reach the relay, **then** deep-link:
+
+```js
+const onStake = async () => {
+  // 1. Kick off the write. Do NOT await-then-redirect — start it, keep the tab alive.
+  const txPromise = writeContractAsync({ /* stake args */ });
+
+  // 2. Foreground the wallet only if this is a mobile WC session (see skip rules).
+  const link = getWalletRedirect(connector); // native/universal from WC session metadata
+  if (link) {
+    setTimeout(() => { window.location.href = link; }, DELAY_MS);
+  }
+
+  // 3. Await the result after the wallet is in front.
+  await txPromise;
+};
+```
+
+The request must be **published before** you background the browser, so the wallet
+foregrounds with the prompt already queued.
+
+> Note: modern RainbowKit + `@walletconnect/*` already do this internally via the
+> session's `redirect` metadata. If you're hand-rolling the deep link, the most
+> robust fix is often to **upgrade and delete the manual redirect**, letting the
+> connector's built-in mobile redirect handle it. The pattern below is for when
+> you must do it yourself.
+
+### The delay and why its length matters
+
+`DELAY_MS` is a compromise between two hard limits:
+
+- **Lower bound (~50–100 ms):** long enough for the connector to serialize,
+  encrypt, and **publish the request onto the relay**. Open the wallet before that
+  and you reproduce the patch's failure (wallet foregrounds with nothing queued).
+- **Upper bound (~a few hundred ms):** short enough to stay inside the browser's
+  **user-activation window**. Wait too long and iOS blocks the app switch — back
+  to "nothing happens." It also must fire before the freeze from backgrounding
+  bites, which is why you never `await` the full tx before redirecting.
+
+A practical value is **~100 ms**. If you can subscribe to a "request published"
+signal from the connector/provider instead of guessing, prefer that over a fixed
+timer — it removes the race entirely. But even then keep the deep link inside the
+gesture-adjacent window; don't wait for the tx to confirm.
+
+### Choosing the wallet scheme (why `connector.id` is useless here)
+
+`connector.id === "walletConnect"` tells you the *transport*, not the *wallet*.
+The same connector serves Rainbow, MetaMask, Trust, Zerion, etc. Hardcoding
+`rainbow://` breaks every non-Rainbow user.
+
+The authoritative source is the **WC session peer metadata**, which carries the
+wallet's own redirect targets:
+
+```js
+function getWalletRedirect(connector) {
+  const provider = connector.getProvider?.();      // WC EthereumProvider
+  const redirect = provider?.session?.peer?.metadata?.redirect;
+  // e.g. { native: "rainbow://", universal: "https://rnbwapp.com" }
+  if (!redirect) return null;
+  return redirect.universal || redirect.native;    // prefer universal on iOS
+}
+```
+
+- Prefer the **universal link** (`https://…`) on iOS: a custom scheme for an
+  uninstalled app throws "Cannot Open Page," whereas a universal link degrades
+  gracefully.
+- Fall back to `native` if no universal link is provided.
+- You can also match `session.peer.metadata.name` against RainbowKit's wallet
+  registry to resolve a link — but reading `redirect` off the session is the
+  direct route.
+
+### When to skip the deep link entirely
+
+Only WC **mobile** sessions need it. Skip it (and let the wallet prompt natively)
+when:
+
+- **Injected / browser-extension connector** (`injected`, `metaMask`, `coinbase`
+  extension): the wallet lives in the same page context and prompts inline.
+  Redirecting to a scheme does nothing useful or actively breaks the flow.
+- **In-app dApp browser** (you're already running *inside* MetaMask's or Coinbase
+  Wallet's browser): there is no separate app to foreground — you'd be deep-linking
+  to yourself.
+- **Desktop**, where WC shows a QR modal: the signing device is elsewhere; there's
+  no local app to open.
+- **No `redirect` in the peer metadata**: some wallets omit it — don't invent a
+  scheme, you'll just error out or open the wrong app.
+- **No active/connected session**: nothing to foreground.
+- **Coinbase Wallet via its own SDK connector** (not WC): it has its own redirect
+  handling; don't layer a WC-style deep link on top.
+
+A concise guard:
+
+```js
+const isMobile = /iPhone|iPad|Android/i.test(navigator.userAgent);
+const isWalletConnect = connector?.id === "walletConnect";
+const shouldDeepLink = isMobile && isWalletConnect && !!getWalletRedirect(connector);
+```
+
+---
+
+## One-line summary
+
+Before: request delivered, wallet not foregrounded (redirect lost after `await`).
+Patch: wallet foregrounded, request never delivered (opened before publish +
+froze the tab). Fix: publish first → short (~100 ms) delay → open the **wallet's
+own** redirect from WC session metadata, and only for live mobile WC sessions.

